| $[3,2,0]$ $(5,4,1)$ | $\begin{pmatrix}13&-6\\-1&22\end{pmatrix}$ | $35,\ 280$ | $(35\mp\sqrt{105})/2$ | $6r^2+9r-1=0$ |
| $[3,2,0]$ $(5,3,2)$ | $\tfrac43\begin{pmatrix}16&-1\\-2&26\end{pmatrix}$ | $56,\ 736$ | $28\mp4\sqrt3$ | $r^2+10r-2=0$ |
@endtable

The octet mixing parameter $R=(e_1E_{13}\cdot C)/(e_1E_{23}E_{12}\cdot C)$ on the weight-$(1,1,1)$ slice satisfies $R^2-5R-5=0$ for the adjoint, with roots $5.854101966250$ and $-0.854101966250$; the Gaudin-extracted values agree to $1.5\cdot10^{-18}$ and $5.7\cdot10^{-20}$. For $[3,1,0]$ $(5,2,1)$ the roots are $4.311737691490$ and $-0.811737691490$ (agreement $5\cdot10^{-19}$), and exactly the same numbers appear for $[3,2,0]$ $(5,4,1)$, as duality requires. Notice that $(5,3,2)$, the sector that needed per-copy cleaning in XXX, has literally the same quadratic as $[3,1,0]$'s $(4,3,1)$, namely $r^2+10r-2=0$. The XXX values at finite $t=\theta_{12}/h$ were different (for example $-1.694$ and $1.096$ for the adjoint octet); the comparison with the $t\to\infty$ limit of those numbers was proposed but not carried out. For $[3,1,0]$ agent W1 did not compute the mixing in the $(4,3,1)$ sector (the functionals $e_1E_{13}$ and $e_1E_{23}E_{12}$ do not suffice there); the $[3,2,0]$ extractions of W3 solve the two-dimensional slices from pairs of grid points.

## Duality $[3,2,0]\cong[3,1,0]^*\otimes\det^3$ {#sec:duality}

The HW sectors of $[3,2,0]^{\otimes2}$ are exactly the dual images $\mu^*=(6-\mu_3,6-\mu_2,6-\mu_1)$ of those of $[3,1,0]^{\otimes2}$, with the same multiplicities: $(6,2,0)\to(6,4,0)$, $(6,1,1)\to(5,5,0)$, $(5,3,0)\to(6,3,1)$, $(5,2,1)\times2\to(5,4,1)\times2$, $(4,4,0)\to(6,2,2)$, $(4,3,1)\times2\to(5,3,2)\times2$, $(4,2,2)\to(4,4,2)$, $(3,3,2)\to(4,3,3)$. The eigenvalues of $\theta_{12}S3c[1,2]$ on a dual sector differ from those of the original by a \emph{sector-dependent scalar} while the traceless parts agree: $(4,3,1)\to(5,3,2)$: $+16$ ($96-24x+x^2\to736-56x+x^2$); $(5,2,1)\to(5,4,1)$: $+7$ ($84-21x+x^2\to280-35x+x^2$); multiplicity one: $(3,3,2)\to(4,3,3)$: $20\to36$, $(4,2,2)\to(4,4,2)$: $18\to30$, $(4,4,0)\to(6,2,2)$: $0\to24$, $(5,3,0)\to(6,3,1)$: $0\to16$, $(6,1,1)\to(5,5,0)$: $8\to0$, $(6,2,0)\to(6,4,0)$: $0\to0$. The scalar shift is new. The G-orthonormal slice ratios agree for $(4,3,1)\leftrightarrow(5,3,2)$ ($-3.6049$ and $0.06935$ in both) but not for $(5,2,1)\leftrightarrow(5,4,1)$, because the GT bases of $V$ and $V^*$ differ by a rotation in a two-dimensional weight space; the basis-independent mixing $R$ above agrees. The exponents of $\det N$, $|R(\omega)|$ and the $\epsilon$-orders transport by duality too ($\epsilon^{16,15,14}$).^[Agents W3 (`Gaudin-L2-chain-320.wb`, duality row of the summary table) and P4 (`RESULTS-Qside-320.md` Q4).]

## The controls and the "no cleaning" answer {#sec:controls}

Three controls test what the family needs.

- $\omega_1'=\omega_1$ (common rotation). Then $a'=g'=0$, the family reads only the top branches: $(4,2,0),(4,1,1)$ for $[2,1,0]$ (full family), $(6,4,0),(6,3,1),(6,2,2)$ for $[3,2,0]$, and only the top branch in the $(\infty,\infty)$ family. The others are "not read". This is as in XXX.
- $\omega_1'$ held fixed at a finite value, not untwisted. In XXX this broke the untwisting. In Gaudin with \emph{finite} $\omega_2$ it does not: $\Omega_D[\zeta\to0,\omega']$ has a finite direction-dependent limit, $K/S$ is still $O(\epsilon)$ on every branch, and the per-weight ranks over six directions are full. We expected the opposite and recorded it as a surprise.
- $\omega_1'$ held fixed in the $(\infty,\infty)$ family (at $\omega_2=\infty$). This \emph{fails}: $\varphi_u$ has rank $\le1$ on the multiplicity-2 weights, singlet-type branches have $K/S=O(1)$ (for $[3,2,0]$: $0.465$ at $(4,4,2)$ and $0.388$ at $(4,3,3)$, identical to $[3,1,0]$'s duals $(4,2,2)$ and $(3,3,2)$), and all branches with a 2-dimensional slice have $O(1)$ CG errors ($0.39$ to $1.5$; $\sigma_2=1.5\cdot10^{-7},1.7\cdot10^{-8},8.7\cdot10^{-15}$). Branches with one-dimensional slices still pass.

So the answer to Paul's question is: no cleaning is needed anywhere, in either family, for the adjoint, $[3,1,0]$ or $[3,2,0]$. Specifically $(5,3,2)$ has $\max|K/S|=1.8\cdot10^{-6},1.4\cdot10^{-6}$ (full family) and $2.4\cdot10^{-6},1.3\cdot10^{-6}$ ($(\infty,\infty)$) at $\epsilon=10^{-6}$, with the same doubling ratio as every other branch. What is untested is $[4,2,0]$ (dimension 27, multiplicity-3 sector $(6,4,2)$), the other XXX trouble spot. The only weakly conditioned $2\times2$ extraction is $(4,4,2)$ in the $(\infty,\infty)$ family ($\sigma_2\sim3\cdot10^{-4}$), still accurate to $1.4\cdot10^{-17}$ at 200 digits; conditioning is a property of the chosen pair of grid points (best pairs have $\sigma_2\approx0.4$ to $2.8$), not of the family.^[Agent W3, `Gaudin-L2-chain-320.wb`, summary "Cleaning verdict (A4)", cells 41--57. The exact slice of $(4,4,2)$ has an exactly vanishing component and the sign convention of the HW kets differs between the exact ket and the chain branch there, so the CG cells align signs and use norm-based errors.]
